d":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The core (`EngineRegistry`), without importing the engine","locale":"en"},"11747":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The implementation: an `Engine` subclass and its `LoadedEngine`, built on `mizuoto.runtime`, `mizuoto.nn` and `mizuoto.text`","locale":"en"},"11748":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"`mizuoto/engines/<id>/`, or `packages/native/<id>/` for a distribution of its own","locale":"en"},"11749":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The runtime, in the server's process","locale":"en"},"11750":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"Tests","locale":"en"},"11751":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"`tests/engines_native/test_<id>_tiny.py`, the contract suite, `scripts/parity/`","locale":"en"},"11752":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"CI, and you","locale":"en"},"11753":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The core plans and validates every request from the manifest alone, then calls the implementation through `core/inference` (see Runtime). This page walks through OmniVoice: `mizuoto/core/engines/manifests/omnivoice.toml` and `mizuoto/engines/omnivoice/`.","locale":"en"},"11754":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`mizuoto/core/engines/manifests/*.toml` ships in the `mizuoto` package; `EngineRegistry` loads every file in the folder and validates it as `EngineManifest` (`mizuoto/core/engines/models.py`). The manifest of a licence-gated engine ships in `mizuoto` too, so the catalogue and the licence text are there before its distribution is installed. The top level:","locale":"en"},"11755":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Field","locale":"en"},"11756":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"11757":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`id`","locale":"en"},"11758":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"The engine id; the implementation is registered under the same id","locale":"en"},"11759":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`devices`","locale":"en"},"11760":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Device kinds the engine runs on (`cuda`, `cpu`, `mps`, `xpu`)","locale":"en"},"11761":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`languages`","locale":"en"},"11762":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Canonical language codes; `*` means any","locale":"en"},"11763":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`[upstream]`","locale":"en"},"11764":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"The project and commit the port reproduces (documentation, not a dependency)","locale":"en"},"11765":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`ops`, `internal`","locale":"en"},"11766":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`call` operation names; a helper engine (ASR) not offered for synthesis","locale":"en"},"11767":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`[weights]`","locale":"en"},"11768":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Roles and suffixes of a voice's own fine-tuned weights (GPT-SoVITS: `gpt = \".ckpt\"`, `sovits = \".pth\"`)","locale":"en"},"11769":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#variants-and-capabilities","content":"A variant is a set of static capabilities; requests are validated against it before any job exists:","locale":"en"},"11770":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#variants-and-capabilities","content":"The full list is `Capability` in `models.py`. Some drive the pipeline: without `long_text` Mizuoto segments the text; `batch` with `max_batch > 1` enables micro-batching; `stream_audio`'s granularity comes from `stream_granularity`; `stream_text_input` allows streamed text over the WebSocket. A variant may also have `languages`, `speakers`, `emotions`, `inline_tags`, `instruct_requires` and `weights_version`.","locale":"en"},"11771":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Table","locale":"en"},"11772":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Model","locale":"en"},"11773":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Notes","locale":"en"},"11774":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`[params.<common name>]`","locale":"en"},"11775":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`ParamSpec`","locale":"en"},"11776":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Only names from `CommonParam` (`temperature`, `top_p`, `num_steps`, `speed`, `seed`…). `target` is the engine's own name; `default` only shows the engine's default, Mizuoto never imposes it; `approximate = true` adds a warning to results; `variants` and `streaming` narrow where it applies","locale":"en"},"11777":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`[options.<name>]`","locale":"en"},"11778":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`OptionSpec`","locale":"en"},"11779":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Engine-specific options (`engine_options`); unknown ones always raise; `group = \"hidden\"` keeps one out of forms. The engine's `OPTION_SCHEMA` must mirror them exactly","locale":"en"},"11780":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`[load_options.<name>]`","locale":"en"},"11781":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`OptionSpec`","locale":"en"},"11782":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Applied when a model loads (`engines.<id>.load_options`, the engine card's Options), such as F5-TTS's `ode_method` or IndexTTS's `use_torch_compile`. They reach the engine in `LoadSpec.options`","locale":"en"},"11783":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#models-and-files-manifests","content":"`files` (`ModelFiles`) may also have `subdir`, `into`, `include`, `exclude` and `unpack`; `extra` lists more repositories; `assets` names shared assets; `vram_mb` is the catalogue's memory figure shown with the model (loading never refuses on it).","locale":"en"},"11784":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#models-and-files-manifests","content":"After adding or changing a model, run `python scripts/dev.py model-manifests omnivoice`. `scripts/model_manifests.py` pins the Hugging Face revision to the current commit and lists every file with its size and SHA-256 (LFS files from their metadata, small files fetched and hashed) into `manifests/files/<manifest>`. Downloads verify against it; `test_catalogue_models_have_files_manifests` checks that every catalogue model has one.","locale":"en"},"11785":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#assets-licence-and-training","content":"**`[[assets]]`**: files several models of an engine share (GPT-SoVITS's encoders and text front-end data, IndexTTS-2's w2v-BERT…), described like models, installed in `<models>/_assets/<engine>/<name>/`. A model names the ones it needs in `assets`; the engine gets their folders as `spec.options[\"assets\"]` (asset id → folder).","locale":"en"},"11786":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#assets-licence-and-training","content":"**`[licence]`**: with `gated = true`, downloading the weights and assets and synthesis need `engines.<id>.accepted_licence == id@version`; `file` names the full text in `manifests/licences/`; `trains_others = false` refuses the outputs as training data for other engines; `notice` shows under results; `commercial` tags outputs.","locale":"en"},"11787":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#assets-licence-and-training","content":"**`[training.<id>]`**: training methods, run by the engine's `TRAINER`; see Trainers.","locale":"en"},"11788":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#recognising-model-folders","content":"`mizuoto model import` identifies the engine and variant from file contents alone: `mizuoto/core/models/detect.py` reads configuration files (OmniVoice: `model_type == \"omnivoice\"` in `config.json`), and the engine's `detect_variant` must agree.","locale":"en"},"11789":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#2-the-implementation","content":"An engine is a package under `mizuoto/engines/<id>/` whose `__init__.py` exports the `Engine` subclass, and whose modules import only what the layer rules allow: `mizuoto.runtime`, `mizuoto.nn`, `mizuoto.text`, `mizuoto.training`, `mizuoto.core.codes`, `mizuoto.core.inference.tasks`, its own package, and numpy, torch and the text libraries; never another engine. Its module imports no torch at the top (`test_engine_contract_import_is_light` and `test_manifest_options_match_the_engines` import engine modules without torch): torch is imported inside the methods, and type hints go under `if TYPE_CHECKING:`.","locale":"en"},"11790":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Member","locale":"en"},"11791":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"11792":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`id`","locale":"en"},"11793":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"The engine id (the manifest's and the registry's)","locale":"en"},"11794":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`VERSION`","locale":"en"},"11795":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"The implementation's own version string (default `\"1\"`). Voice caches are keyed by Mizuoto's version, not this one","locale":"en"},"11796":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`OPTION_SCHEMA`","locale":"en"},"11797":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`OptionSpec`s with exactly the manifest's `[options]`: same type, default, min, max and choices (`test_manifest_options_match_the_engines`)","locale":"en"},"11798":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`TRAINER`","locale":"en"},"11799":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`\"module:Class\"` of the engine's `mizuoto.training.Trainer`, when it trains","locale":"en"},"11800":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`EXTRAS`","locale":"en"},"11801":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Optional features: install spec → the modules it provides, e.g. GPT-SoVITS's `{\"mizuoto[gptsovits-ja]\": (\"pyopenjtalk\",), \"mizuoto[gptsovits-ko]\": (\"mecab\", \"jamo\")}`; `mizuoto doctor` lists the ones that do not import","locale":"en"},"11802":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`detect_variant(model_dir)`","locale":"en"},"11803":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"The variant from the folder's files only, without torch","locale":"en"},"11804":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`load(spec, runtime, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"11805":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Build the model from `spec` (`model_id`, `model_dir`, `variant`, `device`, `dtype`, `options`) and return a `LoadedEngine`; report with `ctx.progress(\"loading:<part>\")`, check `ctx.check_cancel()` between parts","locale":"en"},"11806":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`call(op, payload, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"11807":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"An operation that needs no loaded model","locale":"en"},"11808":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"One loaded model. It is not re-entrant: the job scheduler gives it one request at a time (the `engine:<engine>:<device>` resource).","locale":"en"},"11809":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Method","locale":"en"},"11810":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Required","locale":"en"},"11811":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"11812":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`describe()`","locale":"en"},"11813":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"11814":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"A `Description`: sample rate, capabilities, speakers, languages, VRAM, `ignored_params`","locale":"en"},"11815":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`synthesize(task, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"11816":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"11817":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"A generator: `yield AudioChunk(samples, sample_rate, segment, final)` as soon as each piece is ready, calling `ctx.check_cancel()` at every step; record the values actually used in `ctx.applied` and warnings with `ctx.warn()`","locale":"en"},"11818":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`prepare_voice(voice, cache_dir, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"11819":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Derived voice state (prompt codes, embeddings) written into `cache_dir`, returned as `VoiceCache(files, meta)`; later requests get the files in `VoiceSpec.cache`","locale":"en"},"11820":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`convert(task, ctx)`, `edit(task, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"11821":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Voice conversion and speech editing, generators too; by default they raise `Unsupported`","locale":"en"},"11822":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`call(op, payload, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"11823":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Operations named in the manifest's `ops` (ASR's `transcribe`, Fish Speech's `codec_encode` / `codec_decode`)","locale":"en"},"11824":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`memory_required(task)`","locale":"en"},"11825":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Bytes of device memory a request needs beyond the weights; the model manager makes room for it","locale":"en"},"11826":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`close()`","locale":"en"},"11827":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Drop the weights (the manager has already taken the handles off their devices)","locale":"en"},"11828":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"An engine returns float32 mono audio in `[-1, 1]` at the model's own rate and never encodes: joining, normalising, resampling and encoding are the core's job. It receives resolved, absolute paths that exist (references are 16-bit WAV in `cache/refs/`) and never downloads. The core has validated the request against the manifest, so an engine maps rather than checks.","locale":"en"},"11829":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"The requests and results are standard-library dataclasses in `mizuoto/core/inference/tasks.py`, re-exported by `mizuoto.runtime.engine`:","locale":"en"},"11830":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"Type","locale":"en"},"11831":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"Fields","locale":"en"},"11832":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`SynthesisTask`","locale":"en"},"11833":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`text`, `language`, `voice`, `style` (`instruct`, `emotion`), `params` (common parameters by Mizuoto's names), `options` (engine options), `texts` (a batch), `segment` / `segments`, `stream`, `text_stream`","locale":"en"},"11834":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`VoiceSpec`","locale":"en"},"11835":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`references` (`Reference`: `audio_path`, `transcript`, `language`, `sample_rate`, `duration`), `preset`, `description`, `attributes`, `weights`, `cache`, `voice_id`","locale":"en"},"11836":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`VoiceConversionTask`, `SpeechEditTask`","locale":"en"},"11837":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"A source clip and a voice; a clip, its transcript and `EditSpan`s","locale":"en"},"11838":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`LoadSpec`, `Description`, `AudioChunk`, `VoiceCache`, `OptionSpec`","locale":"en"},"11839":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"What `load` gets, what `describe` returns, the audio, a voice cache, an option","locale":"en"},"11840":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-handles","content":"Every module with weights is wrapped in a `ModelHandle` and listed in `self.handles`, so the model manager can place it, offload it and report its size:","locale":"en"},"11841":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-handles","content":"Build modules from `ops.default` (no random initialisation), load weights with `mizuoto.runtime.loading` (`load_state_dict`, `load_into`, strict), and leave parameters as `nn.Module` makes them. Draw every random number from `ctx.generator_for(device)` or `ctx.rng`, in upstream's order (see Runtime).","locale":"en"},"11842":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-handles","content":"Compute attention with `mizuoto.runtime.accel.attention(q, k, v, site=SITE, …)`, never `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` (an architecture test forbids it): declare `SITE = accel.Site(\"<id>.<part>\")` once per call site, and pass the arguments upstream passed to SDPA (`causal` is its `is_causal`). A site where upstream wrote attention by hand gives that code as its `reference`; one that only its reference may compute is `swappable=False` (attentions with relative positions inside the scores are not sites at all: they stay hand-written). Where the site builds a mask from a structure (padding on one side, causal, a window), pass `mask_info` too, so faster kernels can take the call (Acceleration).","locale":"en"},"11843":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#errors","content":"Raise `EngineError(message, detail, code=codes.…)` with a code from `mizuoto.core.codes` (`invalid_input`, `capability_not_supported`, `model_missing`…); put the fixing command in `detail[\"hint\"]`. `Unsupported(message)` is `capability_not_supported`. The core rebuilds the domain error from the code; running out of memory and other exceptions are mapped for you.","locale":"en"},"11844":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"The runtime finds an implementation by id (`get_engine` in `mizuoto/runtime/engine.py`):","locale":"en"},"11845":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"**In this distribution**: add the engine to `BUILTIN`:","locale":"en"},"11846":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"**In a distribution of its own** (a licence that cannot ship in `mizuoto`, decision D1): register the class under the entry-point group `mizuoto.engines` in its `pyproject.toml`, depend on `mizuoto`, and add the engine id → distribution name to `DISTRIBUTIONS` in `mizuoto/core/inference/service.py`, so a missing install is reported by name. Fish Speech's:","locale":"en"},"11847":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"Entry points are read once per process: installing such a distribution means restarting Mizuoto. Its package follows the engines' rules and mirrors its manifest (`check_native_package`, `check_options_mirror`, called by its `tests/test_<id>_package.py`), has its own version in `mizuoto_<id>/version.py` with the Mizuoto versions it works with in its `pyproject.toml` (`check_gated_distribution`), is checked by CI's `gated` and `oldest-mizuoto` jobs and is built by `scripts/build_wheels.py native` (see Release).","locale":"en"},"11848":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"An engine is *available* when torch imports (the dummy engine needs none) and its implementation is registered; otherwise `EngineStatus.missing` names what is missing and the UI and `mizuoto doctor` show the fix.","locale":"en"},"11849":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"`tests/engines_native/test_<id>_tiny.py` builds a tiny model of the engine's own architecture with random weights in a temporary folder (config files, a byte-level vocabulary, safetensors written by the test) and drives the real code path on the CPU: loading, voice prompts and their cache, decoding, batches, seeds that repeat, cancellation. No weights are downloaded, so CI runs it on every push (CPU torch). `test_omnivoice_tiny.py` is a complete example; trainers have `test_<id>_train_tiny.py`.","locale":"en"},"11850":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"`tests/engines/test_contract.py` (markers `engine` and `weights`, deselected by default) drives the whole stack (plan → job → runtime → engine) for every installed catalogue model of the available engines: `describe` agrees with the manifest; every declared voice mode is audible, at the right rate, about as long as the text needs, and says the text (word error rate below 0.35 when ASR is installed); a seed repeats; two segments split; cancelling mid-generation stops the engine; and no process-wide state is left behind. A new engine id goes into the suite's `engines` settings override. See Testing.","locale":"en"},"11851":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"Mizuoto's standard for a port is identical output to the upstream implementation on the CPU in fp32 under a seed. For the engines Mizuoto 0.1 had, `scripts/parity/` runs the same cases through 0.1's stack and the native one and compares them sample for sample (see Testing). Record what the port reproduces, where it departs and the parity result on its page in the engine reference (`site/content/docs/engines-<id>.mdx` and `.en.mdx`), and any rule a later change must not break in `AGENTS.md`.","locale":"en"},"11852":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"Look at random numbers first: upstream may draw them while building a model or preparing a reference (HuBERT's layer drop, IndexTTS's w2v-BERT, CosyVoice 3's vocoder noise), or with tensors of other strides. Then at tensor shapes and kernels: SDPA with or without a mask, a mask's dtype, contiguity, the CPU thread count. `AGENTS.md` §2b lists such findings engine by engine.","locale":"en"},"11853":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`mizuoto/engines/omnivoice/` (about 2 900 lines in all):","locale":"en"},"11854":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"Module","locale":"en"},"11855":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"What","locale":"en"},"11856":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`engine.py`","locale":"en"},"11857":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`OmniVoiceEngine` and `LoadedOmniVoice`: loading, voice prompts, the iterative decoding, chunked long text, decoding and post-processing","locale":"en"},"11858":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`model.py`","locale":"en"},"11859":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"The diffusion language model on `mizuoto.runtime.lm.qwen`'s decoder","locale":"en"},"11860":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`audio_tokenizer.py`","locale":"en"},"11861":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"The HiggsAudio v2 tokenizer: `nn/codecs/dac`, `nn/ssl/hubert`, residual codebooks","locale":"en"},"11862":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`audio.py`, `text.py`, `duration.py`, `voice_design.py`, `lang_map.py`","locale":"en"},"11863":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"Silence removal and fades as pydub computes them; text chunking and punctuation; the duration estimate; instructions from design attributes; language names","locale":"en"},"11864":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`train.py`","locale":"en"},"11865":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`OmniVoiceTrainer`: methods `full` and `lora`","locale":"en"},"11866":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"What it shows:","locale":"en"},"11867":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Loading**: `OmniVoiceModel(config, ops.default)` filled by `load_into(model, load_state_dict(folder / \"model.safetensors\"))`, cast to the dtype (bf16 on a GPU that has it, else fp16; fp32 on the CPU; an explicit `engines.omnivoice.dtype` wins); the audio tokenizer the same way; the text tokenizer from `mizuoto.text.tokenize.bpe.load_bpe(folder)`; two handles.","locale":"en"},"11868":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Parameter mapping**: Mizuoto's common names map onto upstream's generation config (`num_steps → num_step`, `temperature → class_temperature`), engine options fill the rest.","locale":"en"},"11869":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Three voice modes**: references → a voice prompt; attributes → an instruction from `instruct_from(attributes)`; neither → the model's own voice. A free-text description raises `capability_not_supported`.","locale":"en"},"11870":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-147","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Voice cache**: `prepare_voice` saves the prompt as `prompt.pt`; `synthesize` prefers `voice.cache[\"prompt\"]`.","locale":"en"},"11871":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-148","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Randomness**: the Gumbel noise draws from `ctx.generator_for(self.device)`; HuBERT's layer-drop draws (made even in eval mode upstream) from `ctx.generator_for(\"cpu\")`.","locale":"en"},"11872":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-149","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Cancel and progress**: `ctx.check_cancel()` at every decoding step, `ctx.progress(\"generating\", …)` after it.","locale":"en"},"11873":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-150","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Batches**: with `task.texts` set, every text in one batch, each yielded with `segment = task.segment + index`.","locale":"en"},"11874":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-151","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Memory**: `memory_required` estimates the logits' working memory per batch row.","locale":"en"},"11875":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-152","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"OmniVoice's engine page records the parity result (identical to 0.1 on the CPU in fp32 for cloning, design, its own voice and the fine-tuned model) and what exactness took.","locale":"en"},"11876":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-153","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"`mizuoto/core/engines/manifests/<id>.toml`; run `dev.py model-manifests <id>`; the licence text in `manifests/licences/` when gated.","locale":"en"},"11877":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-154","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"`mizuoto/engines/<id>/` (or `packages/native/<id>/`): the `Engine` subclass with `OPTION_SCHEMA` mirroring the manifest, its `LoadedEngine`, and the modules it needs; shared parts into `mizuoto/nn` or `mizuoto/runtime`.","locale":"en"},"11878":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-155","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"Registration: `BUILTIN` in `mizuoto/runtime/engine.py`, or the `mizuoto.engines` entry point plus `DISTRIBUTIONS`. `mizuoto/core/models/detect.py` recognises its model folders; optional packages go in an extra of `pyproject.toml` and in `EXTRAS`.","locale":"en"},"11879":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-156","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"Tests: `tests/engines_native/test_<id>_tiny.py` (an engine in its own distribution keeps them in its package's `tests/`, as `packages/native/<pkg>/tests/` do); the engine id list in `tests/core/test_engines.py::test_registry_lookups` and the `engines` override in `tests/engines/test_contract.py`.","locale":"en"},"11880":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-157","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"`mizuoto model download <id>/<name>`, then the contract suite on the real models; record the port and its parity on the engine's page (`engines-<id>`, both languages), add it to the sidebars (`meta.json`, `meta.en.json`) and to the engine reference table, and add rules to `AGENTS.md` if there are any.","locale":"en"},"11881":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"page","content":"Memory","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory","locale":"en"},"11882":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"How the runtime spends GPU memory and RAM, after ComfyUI: phases, the budget and VRAM modes, units and weight streaming, host copies, partial loading and eviction, the exact out-of-memory retry, working-memory estimates and their calibration, simulated devices, training presets, and what an engine does to take part.","locale":"en"},"11883":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Where it lives","locale":"en"},"11884":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"Phases","locale":"en"},"11885":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"The budget","locale":"en"},"11886":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Units: what streams","locale":"en"},"11887":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"Weight streaming","locale":"en"},"11888":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"Host copies and RAM","locale":"en"},"11889":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Eviction","locale":"en"},"11890":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Running out of memory","locale":"en"},"11891":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"Working-memory estimates","locale":"en"},"11892":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"Reporting","locale":"en"},"11893":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#static-kv-cache-and-the-cuda-allocator","content":"Static KV cache and the CUDA allocator","locale":"en"},"11894":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"Simulated devices and tests","locale":"en"},"11895":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Training","locale":"en"},"11896":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"Writing an engine for it","locale":"en"},"11897":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"A model runs whenever its smallest working set fits the device, not only when all of it fits. Mizuoto's runtime does this the way ComfyUI does (`load_models_gpu`, `ModelPatcher`'s partial loading, dynamic VRAM), without a native allocator: a request places only the components its current step needs; when they do not fit, other components give up memory unit by unit; when that is still not enough, the components load partly and the rest of their weights stream from RAM as each layer runs. Running out of memory becomes slower, not failed.","locale":"en"},"11898":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"None of this changes a result. A streamed layer computes with the same tensors as a resident one, a retried step replays the same random draws, and the tests run every engine with its weights streamed and compare the audio with the plain run. The mechanisms that do change numbers (tiled decoding, the static KV cache, the `lowest` training preset) are opt-in and say so in the output.","locale":"en"},"11899":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"The user-facing settings are in Performance; the runtime as a whole in Runtime.","locale":"en"},"11900":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Part","locale":"en"},"11901":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Code","locale":"en"},"11902":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Role","locale":"en"},"11903":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`VramPolicy`","locale":"en"},"11904":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/policy.py`","locale":"en"},"11905":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"The mode, reserves, host copies, pinned budget, copy streams and OOM switches, built from the `compute` settings at every use (`core/inference/service.py: policy_from`), so a change reaches the next load without a restart","locale":"en"},"11906":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Units","locale":"en"},"11907":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/units.py`","locale":"en"},"11908":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Which parts of a module may stay off the device and which are fixed","locale":"en"},"11909":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`ModelHandle`","locale":"en"},"11910":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/handle.py`","locale":"en"},"11911":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"One component: whole or partly placed, host copies, hooks for streamed units","locale":"en"},"11912":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`Streamer`","locale":"en"},"11913":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/stream.py`","locale":"en"},"11914":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"One per device: the device copies of streamed units, their learnt order, prefetch on side streams, pinning","locale":"en"},"11915":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`ModelManager`","locale":"en"},"11916":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/manager.py`","locale":"en"},"11917":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Every registered component, the budget, eviction, partial loading, the OOM retry's clean-up, calibration, reporting","locale":"en"},"11918":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`RequestContext.use` / `run`","locale":"en"},"11919":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/context.py`","locale":"en"},"11920":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"A phase of a request; `run` adds the exact retry and the fallback","locale":"en"},"11921":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`peak_bytes`, `BufferCache`","locale":"en"},"11922":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/lm/cache.py`","locale":"en"},"11923":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"KV cache peaks for estimates; the static cache","locale":"en"},"11924":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`tiled_1d`","locale":"en"},"11925":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/nn/dsp/tiles.py`","locale":"en"},"11926":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Decoding in overlapping tiles (a fallback)","locale":"en"},"11927":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`DeviceService`","locale":"en"},"11928":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/device.py`","locale":"en"},"11929":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Free memory, `vram_limit_mb`, simulated devices","locale":"en"},"11930":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Training tools","locale":"en"},"11931":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/training/memory.py`","locale":"en"},"11932":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Checkpointing, presets, host optimizer, streamed frozen weights, estimates","locale":"en"},"11933":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"An engine with `PHASED = True` (every real engine Mizuoto ships; the dummy is not) wraps each part of a request in a phase, so only that part's components are placed, with that part's working memory set aside:","locale":"en"},"11934":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"**`ctx.use(handles, memory)`** is a context manager: the manager loads `handles` (whole or partly) with room for `memory` bytes of working memory and keeps them from giving up memory until the block ends. Use it for a phase that yields audio or consumes a generator.","locale":"en"},"11935":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"**`ctx.run(name, handles, fn, memory, generators=(), fallback=None)`** is the same around a pure function and adds the exact retry and the fallback (below). Use it wherever the block's only effect is its result.","locale":"en"},"11936":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"Components the phase does not name stay where they are and give up memory first when another phase needs it: an IndexTTS clone of a cached voice never places w2v-BERT or CAM++.","locale":"en"},"11937":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"An engine without `PHASED` gets every handle for the whole call, with `memory_required(task)` as the working memory.","locale":"en"},"11938":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"On the CPU the manager moves and counts nothing (the load and offload device are the same), so phases cost nothing there.","locale":"en"},"11939":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`ModelManager.load(handles, memory)` decides per device:","locale":"en"},"11940":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**Need** = the handles' bytes not yet on the device + the working memory (the estimate times its calibration, below) + `compute.vram_reserve_mb`.","locale":"en"},"11941":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**Free** = the driver's free memory plus what torch's caching allocator holds but does not use, capped by `compute.vram_limit_mb` (less what this process already holds) when that is set.","locale":"en"},"11942":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"If free memory falls short, other idle components give up memory (eviction, below), then free memory is measured again.","locale":"en"},"11943":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"If the handles fit, they load whole. Otherwise they load partly within the budget: free memory less working memory and reserve.","locale":"en"},"11944":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"Mode (`compute.vram_mode`, `engines.<id>.vram_mode`)","locale":"en"},"11945":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"Whole when it fits","locale":"en"},"11946":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"Otherwise","locale":"en"},"11947":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`auto` / `normal`","locale":"en"},"11948":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"11949":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"partly, down to the minimum","locale":"en"},"11950":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`high`","locale":"en"},"11951":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"11952":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"whole anyway, after idle components left whole; no partial loading","locale":"en"},"11953":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`low`","locale":"en"},"11954":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"only when the weights take at most half of the device's memory (`low_ratio`)","locale":"en"},"11955":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"partly, within that half","locale":"en"},"11956":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`none`","locale":"en"},"11957":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"never","locale":"en"},"11958":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"every unit streams: the fixed parts and the copies in flight only","locale":"en"},"11959":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`shared` (automatic on MPS)","locale":"en"},"11960":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"11961":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"whole: unified memory gains nothing from offloading, so no partial loading and no host copies","locale":"en"},"11962":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**No estimate refuses a load** (ComfyUI's way). In `high` the model is tried whole once idle components made room; in the other modes, below the minimum, it is tried at the minimum with a warning in the log. Only an allocation that really fails is an error. The catalogue's `vram_mb` is never checked before loading.","locale":"en"},"11963":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**Two requests on one GPU.** With `compute.gpu_jobs` above 1, a request short of its minimum (in `high`: of its whole size) while another request holds components on the device waits for that request to let go, up to 10 minutes, checking for a cancel; then it tries.","locale":"en"},"11964":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A unit is a group of weights that moves together: a decoder layer, a DiT block, a vocoder stage (ComfyUI's dynamic-VRAM units).","locale":"en"},"11965":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A module may name its units with `memory_units() -> list[nn.Module]`. Otherwise each child of a `ModuleList` or `Sequential` of at least 1 MB is a unit (the outermost lists; a unit is never split further).","locale":"en"},"11966":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"**Only the weights of `nn.Linear`, `Conv1d/2d` and `ConvTranspose1d/2d` stream**, and of classes that declare `STREAMABLE = True` (IndexTTS's GPT-2 `Conv1D`). Their `forward` is where they are read.","locale":"en"},"11967":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"**Everything else is fixed**: on the device whenever the component is. That includes embeddings (codebooks are read through `.weight` as often as they are called), norms, scales, buffers, a parameter shared with a module outside the unit (tied embeddings and heads), the model's first parameter (code reads the model's device from it), units under 64 KB, modules marked `fixed` and everything under a module pinned to the CPU (`DeviceService.pin_to_cpu`, which never moves).","locale":"en"},"11968":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"An engine marks submodules on the handle with `handle.mark(module, mark)`:","locale":"en"},"11969":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Mark","locale":"en"},"11970":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"11971":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Used by","locale":"en"},"11972":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`fixed`","locale":"en"},"11973":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Never streams: code reads its weights outside its `forward`","locale":"en"},"11974":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"CosyVoice's HiFT F0 predictor; GPT-SoVITS's T2S (`memory_units() = []`)","locale":"en"},"11975":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`hot`","locale":"en"},"11976":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Runs many times per step: resident first","locale":"en"},"11977":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Qwen3-TTS's code predictor, VoxCPM's local DiT and encoder, Whisper's decoder","locale":"en"},"11978":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`cold`","locale":"en"},"11979":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Runs once per request: streams first","locale":"en"},"11980":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A handle's `kind` decides which units stay resident when not all fit, after the `hot` ones:","locale":"en"},"11981":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`sequence` (an autoregressive LM, every layer once per token): units in execution order, so a fixed prefix of layers stays and the same layers stream every token;","locale":"en"},"11982":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`oneshot` (encoders, decoders, flow steps over a whole input): the largest first, which keeps the copies in flight small.","locale":"en"},"11983":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A handle's **minimum** is its fixed parts plus the copies of its largest units the streamer holds at once.","locale":"en"},"11984":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"A streamed unit's weights rest in RAM. Forward pre- and post-hooks on the unit, and on each module holding its weights, swap `param.data` to a device copy for the call and back after it. Hooks on the leaves make any path into a layer work (`layer.forward_step(…)`, `vae.decode`), and nothing in `ops` or in a layer's code changes. A component wholly on its device has no hooks at all, so the resident path costs nothing.","locale":"en"},"11985":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"The device's `Streamer`:","locale":"en"},"11986":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**keeps a few copies**: the running unit and those in flight (`compute.async_offload + 1`); a unit that runs again before its copy is dropped (CFG halves, beams) reuses it;","locale":"en"},"11987":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**learns the order** units run in (each unit's successor, as last seen) and, on CUDA and ROCm, **prefetches** the next units on side streams while the current one computes. The compute stream waits on a copy's event, and each copy is recorded on the compute stream so the caching allocator does not reuse it early. A unit entered out of order is copied there and then: correctness never depends on the order;","locale":"en"},"11988":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**does not prefetch for `sequence` handles** (autoregressive models): their units hold one copy, and the placement sets aside one unit's room for them, not `async_offload + 1`. A layer's work for one token hides none of its copy, so prefetching there only took room from resident weights: on an RTX 3090, partly loaded, Qwen3-TTS's talker ran 24.6 s with two side streams and 16.2 s without, VoxCPM 2 29.0 s and 13.6 s, CosyVoice 2 10.1 s and 7.4 s, while the models run once per input (F5-TTS, OmniVoice) are faster with them;","locale":"en"},"11989":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**pins** the host copies of streamed weights (page-locked memory makes copies asynchronous and about twice as fast) within `compute.max_pinned_mb`, by default ComfyUI's budget: 40 % of RAM on Windows, elsewhere `max(0.4 × RAM, min(0.9 × RAM, RAM − 4 GB))`. Beyond it weights stay pageable and copy synchronously. XPU and MPS copy synchronously.","locale":"en"},"11990":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**Pinning in place** (`mizuoto/runtime/pinning.py`, ComfyUI's `pin_memory`). On NVIDIA GPUs a host copy is page-locked where it is (`cudaHostRegister`, 14 GB/s, nothing copied) rather than copied into page-locked memory (`Tensor.pin_memory()`, 1.5 GB/s: a talker's first streamed request paid 0.65–1.0 s for it). The same budget covers the host copies a wholly loaded component keeps (`keep_host`): they are pinned as it loads, so each later reload copies asynchronously at the link's speed. Only whole storages of at least 1 MB are registered (smaller ones share pages with other allocations, and registrations may not overlap); the streamer still copies smaller streamed tensors into page-locked memory, since a pageable copy on a side stream would stall the host. A registration is tied to its storage: a finalizer, run when the storage dies and before its memory is freed, waits for the device and unregisters it (a later allocation at the same address would otherwise be taken for page-locked memory). Elsewhere, ROCm included, streamed weights are copied into page-locked memory as before and kept host copies stay pageable.","locale":"en"},"11991":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"Copies are made outside inference mode, in the weights' own dtype, so a streamed layer computes with the same kind of tensor as a resident one (an inference tensor takes other CPU kernels). Each fetch checks for a cancel. A `Compiled` module above a streamed unit runs eagerly (its graph would hold the weights), and CUDA graphs are captured for models wholly on their device, or for a `sequence` handle whose streamed units rest in page-locked memory (`ModelHandle.graphable`; Qwen3-TTS's talker uses it). Captured, each streamed weight's copy is a memcpy node from its fixed host tensor into the graph's own memory, made inside the capture (a copy from the eager run before it is not used: the graph would keep reading that buffer after it is freed) and per leaf, so a layer split across graphs around its attention copies each weight once a step. The streamer counts the replays' copies (`ModelHandle.replayed`), so `meta.memory.streamed_mb` stays right.","locale":"en"},"11992":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"With host copies (`compute.host_copies`: `auto` keeps them while free RAM stays above `compute.ram_reserve_mb`; `on`; `off`), a parameter placed on the device keeps its host tensor, so offloading it costs no transfer: the device is a cache over RAM. A partly loaded component always keeps them (its streamed units rest there). Buffers are always copied back (a module may update one in place).","locale":"en"},"11993":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"Unloading frees everything without copying anything back.","locale":"en"},"11994":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"Loading never holds a float32 copy of a model that runs in half precision: an engine casts the model before it copies the checkpoint in (its parameters are still unwritten, so the cast costs nothing), the same values as loading in float32 and casting after. Qwen3-TTS 1.7B's load fell from 7.4 to 4.2 GB of RAM (11.1 to 5.5 GB resident, host copies off, 2026-10-19); F5-TTS, VoxCPM, Fish Speech and OmniVoice load this way too. Safetensors checkpoints are memory-mapped, so their pages count as resident while read but are page cache the system can drop.","locale":"en"},"11995":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"A weight that changes after placement goes through the handle (`handle.replace(module)`: a voice's own GPT-SoVITS weights), so a host copy is never stale.","locale":"en"},"11996":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"`compute.ram_reserve_mb` (empty: the larger of 2 GB and a tenth of the RAM): below it the runtime service drops the host copies of wholly loaded components first, then unloads idle models, least recently used first, never pinned or busy ones (`RuntimeService.relieve_ram`).","locale":"en"},"11997":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"When free memory falls short, idle components on the same device give up the shortfall, in this order:","locale":"en"},"11998":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"partly loaded ones (already slow; ComfyUI's first key);","locale":"en"},"11999":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"then those of models not in `engines.<id>.pinned_models` before pinned ones;","locale":"en"},"12000":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"then the least recently used.","locale":"en"},"12001":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Components in use by any request are never victims; the running model's components that the current phase does not need are. Each victim gives up units, in reverse fill order, until the shortfall is met (`partially_unload`); in `high` and `shared` it leaves whole. `empty_cache` runs after an eviction, never per request.","locale":"en"},"12002":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Partly loading a phase's components: the smaller ones load whole first, each leaving the others at least their minimum, and the largest gets what is left, its remaining units streaming.","locale":"en"},"12003":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Above the manager, `RuntimeService` keeps at most `compute.max_resident_models` models loaded, unloads idle ones after `compute.unload_after_minutes` (server only), moves every idle model off a GPU before a fine-tune there (`release_device`), and after a job fails for running out of memory unloads every model not in use from every GPU (`ComputeService.release_after_oom`, ComfyUI's `unload_all_models`). See Runtime.","locale":"en"},"12004":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"**The exact retry** (`compute.oom_retry`, on). When a phase run through `ctx.run` runs out of device memory:","locale":"en"},"12005":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the attempt's exception frame is left first (it holds the failed attempt's tensors);","locale":"en"},"12006":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"`manager.after_oom`: every other idle component on the device leaves it, the streamer's cached copies go, and each of the phase's components is capped at half of what it had on the device (`ModelHandle.cap_bytes`, kept until it is offloaded, so the next requests do not run out too);","locale":"en"},"12007":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the request's generators, its `rng` and any the engine names (`generators=`) are put back as they were before the first attempt;","locale":"en"},"12008":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the phase runs again: the same draws, the same result, slower. At most two retries; the output records `applied.oom_retries` and a warning.","locale":"en"},"12009":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"A phase under `ctx.use` (one that yields audio or consumes a generator) is not retried, so its estimate must bound what it can reach.","locale":"en"},"12010":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"**Fallbacks** (`compute.oom_fallback`, off). After the retries an engine's cheaper way runs, if it gave one (`ctx.run(…, fallback=(what, fn))`). Its output differs slightly, so it is recorded in `applied.fallbacks` with a warning:","locale":"en"},"12011":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Engine","locale":"en"},"12012":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Fallback","locale":"en"},"12013":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"ASR","locale":"en"},"12014":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"one 30 s window at a time","locale":"en"},"12015":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"GPT-SoVITS","locale":"en"},"12016":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"one text at a time","locale":"en"},"12017":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"F5-TTS","locale":"en"},"12018":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Vocos in tiles","locale":"en"},"12019":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"IndexTTS","locale":"en"},"12020":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"BigVGAN in tiles","locale":"en"},"12021":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"VoxCPM","locale":"en"},"12022":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the VAE in tiles","locale":"en"},"12023":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"`tiled_1d` decodes 256 frames at a time with 32 frames of overlap, cross-faded (ComfyUI's audio VAE tiling). Qwen3-TTS's codec already decodes in chunks; CosyVoice's HiFT carries a phase that tiles would break.","locale":"en"},"12024":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"A phase's estimate decides whether its weights fit whole, so it is sized by the request (its text tokens, batch rows, the reference's length), not by the worst case `max_new_tokens` allows. Setting aside memory the request never uses pushes weights out for nothing: a worst case of 8192 frames, doubled, was 2.4 GB for a Qwen3-TTS sentence that uses about 20 MB.","locale":"en"},"12025":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"Language models' caches are sized by the text: Qwen3-TTS 16 frames a text token, IndexTTS 24 codes, GPT-SoVITS 16 a phone, CosyVoice upstream's 20 a token, each within `max_new_tokens`; Fish Speech's batches by their true bound; in the dtype and KV width the model computes in.","locale":"en"},"12026":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"`runtime.lm.cache.peak_bytes(position_bytes, layers, positions, kind, rows)` gives a growing cache's peak: for `dynamic`, every layer plus one layer's new copy while a step concatenates (each layer's old tensors are freed before the next), not twice the cache; for `static`, the doubled buffers plus one old buffer while it grows.","locale":"en"},"12027":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"A rare generation that outruns its estimate under `ctx.run` runs again with more weights streamed, exactly.","locale":"en"},"12028":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"**Calibration.** After each phase on CUDA or XPU, the manager measures its working memory: the peak above the placed weights, less the streamer's copies at their most and less what the phase keeps afterwards (CUDA graphs' pools, buffers kept with the model). When that exceeds the estimate, it remembers the ratio (times 1.1, at most 16) per phase, keyed by the engine and its components' names (`qwen3tts:talker`), and sets aside that much more next time. The ratios are kept in `<cache>/memory/calibration.json` (format version 2; a file of another version is ignored).","locale":"en"},"12029":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-147","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"Every output's `meta.memory`: `streamed_mb` (copied from RAM during the request), `loaded_mb`, `size_mb`, and for partly loaded components `partial`: per component the `reason` (the budget fell short, `vram_mode low` or `none`, capped after running out of memory, or gave up memory for another), the `mode`, `free_mb`, `working_mb` and `reserve_mb` weighed, `loaded_mb`, `size_mb` and `gave_up_for`. The log says the same when it happens.","locale":"en"},"12030":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-148","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"`GET /api/v1/runtime` and `mizuoto runtime`: per model `loaded_bytes`, `streamed_bytes` and `host_bytes`; the web UI's loaded-models sheet draws them as a bar.","locale":"en"},"12031":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-149","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"`mizuoto doctor` has a `memory` finding with the policy in effect (mode, reserve, limit, RAM reserve, host copies, pinned budget, streams, retry and fallbacks).","locale":"en"},"12032":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-150","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#static-kv-cache-and-the-cuda-allocator","content":"`compute.kv_cache = static` gives the Qwen-family caches (Qwen3-TTS, CosyVoice 2/3) preallocated buffers that double when full (`BufferCache`): no per-step concatenation, no transient copy, less fragmentation. Attention reads views with the buffer's strides, which some kernels sum in another order. Identical on the CPU; opt-in until it is checked identical on a GPU.","locale":"en"},"12033":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-151","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#static-kv-cache-and-the-cuda-allocator","content":"`compute.cuda_allocator` (`native`, `async`: cudaMallocAsync, `expandable`: expandable segments; `auto` is `native`) is set by the host in `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF` before CUDA is first used, never by engine code, and handed to training stages in their environment (`allocator_env`), whether or not the host has loaded a model. A value the environment already sets wins; a change applies at the next start. Under `async` no CUDA graphs are captured (`graphs.usable`): the stream-ordered allocator frees a capture's warm-up temporaries in a way that made replays fault with an illegal memory access.","locale":"en"},"12034":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-152","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"`DeviceService(simulate={\"cpu\": bytes}, strict=True)` (the setting `compute.simulate_vram_mb`, for tests and the parity harness) makes the CPU play a GPU of that size. The manager places weights by copying them, streams units, and counts what it placed against the simulated size. In strict mode every weight off the device rests as a meta tensor, so:","locale":"en"},"12035":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-153","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"a component used outside its phase fails (it was not placed);","locale":"en"},"12036":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-154","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"a unit's weight read outside its `forward` fails (it is not there).","locale":"en"},"12037":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-155","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"`tests/engines_native/streaming.py` runs each tiny engine so, with every unit streamed (mode `none`), with half the room between its minimum and its size, and whole, and compares each output with the plain CPU run: identical. The parity harness does the same on real models (`--setting compute.simulate_vram_mb=…`). See Testing.","locale":"en"},"12038":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-156","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Training stages run in their own process and own their device: before a GPU stage the host moves every idle model off that GPU, and the train job holds every slot of `gpu:<dev>` (`JobSpec.exclusive`). Inside the stage, `training.memory` chooses a preset of `mizuoto/training/memory.py`'s tools; a method's own hyperparameters win:","locale":"en"},"12039":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-157","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Preset","locale":"en"},"12040":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-158","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Adds","locale":"en"},"12041":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-159","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Results","locale":"en"},"12042":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-160","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"`standard` (default)","locale":"en"},"12043":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-161","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"nothing: each recipe exactly","locale":"en"},"12044":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-162","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"identical to upstream","locale":"en"},"12045":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-163","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"`low`","locale":"en"},"12046":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-164","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"gradient checkpointing over each block (`gradient_checkpointing`: `blocks`, `every:k`; VoxCPM, Qwen3-TTS, OmniVoice, F5-TTS, CosyVoice), the recomputation with the global generators *and the trainer's own* put back; F5-TTS's EMA copy in RAM; VoxCPM's LoRA frozen weights in the autocast dtype","locale":"en"},"12047":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-165","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"identical (tested per trainer on the CPU)","locale":"en"},"12048":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-166","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"`lowest`","locale":"en"},"12049":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-167","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"also AdamW's state in RAM, stepped on the CPU (`host_optimizer`; Qwen3-TTS, OmniVoice, F5-TTS without a gradient scaler); VoxCPM's LoRA base weights streamed from RAM for each product (`streamed_linear`)","locale":"en"},"12050":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-168","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"the optimizer's sums may differ in the last bits on a GPU","locale":"en"},"12051":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-169","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"The host optimizer keeps the float32 copies and the gradients in one buffer each and moves a step's gradients and parameters through one page-locked buffer per device dtype, with one wait; most of its step is AdamW's own arithmetic on the CPU (Qwen3-TTS 1.7B: about 4.5 s of its 5.5 s step), which only a fused optimizer, with other results, would shorten. A `lowest` stage runs without torch's huge pages (`compute.cpu_allocator`): with them its per-step allocations stalled in the kernel's compaction and the run took 2.2× as long.","locale":"en"},"12052":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-170","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Always: a GPU stage reports `memory/peak_mb`; `MethodInfo.memory_mb` estimates a run's peak per preset (`core/training/estimate.py`): every method was measured on an RTX 3090 with its recipe's default batch, each installed base model and each preset; an estimate is the nearest measured model's peak scaled by size, a tenth more and 512 MB for the CUDA context, and for Fish Speech, GPT-SoVITS and VoxCPM, whose peaks follow the batch, the run's `batch_size` against the measured one. A GPU run warns in its log when the estimate exceeds the free memory, naming the preset that would fit or, where none does, the largest batch that would. Trainers themselves are in Trainers.","locale":"en"},"12053":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-171","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Wrap every module with weights in a `ModelHandle`** and list it in `self.handles`. Give an autoregressive LM `kind=\"sequence\"`; mark parts that run many times per step `hot`, and modules whose weights code reads outside their `forward` `fixed`. Name units with `memory_units()` when the default split (children of `ModuleList`/`Sequential`) does not fit the model.","locale":"en"},"12054":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-172","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Never move modules yourself.** Build on the host (or on `spec.device`; the handle takes it from there), and let the manager place them. A float64 module on a device without float64 uses `float64_device` and `pin_to_cpu`. Swap weights with `handle.replace`.","locale":"en"},"12055":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-173","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Set `PHASED = True` and run each part in a phase**: `ctx.run` for pure blocks, `ctx.use` for blocks that yield or consume a generator. Pass `generators=` for any generator the engine owns besides the context's, so a retry replays it.","locale":"en"},"12056":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-174","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Estimate each phase's working memory from the request**: KV caches with `peak_bytes` in the dtype the model computes in, attention scores, CFG batches, decoder activations by length. Under `ctx.use`, bound what the phase can reach.","locale":"en"},"12057":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-175","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Offer a fallback** where a cheaper exact-enough way exists (tiles, one item at a time), with a short description of what it does.","locale":"en"},"12058":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-176","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Run the tiny model under `streaming.py`**: every unit streamed, half, none, strict. A failure there names the component used outside its phase or the weight read outside its unit.","locale":"en"},"12059":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"page","content":"Performance record","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance","locale":"en"},"12060":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance","content":"How Mizuoto's speed is measured under the rule that results never change (perf.py, train_perf.py), the conditions of each measurement, and what each round of work changed: the CPU rounds, the attention dispatcher, CUDA graphs per engine and torch.compile.","locale":"en"},"12061":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"How it is measured","locale":"en"},"12062":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#conditions","content":"Conditions","locale":"en"},"12063":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Where the time goes on the CPU","locale":"en"},"12064":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"CPU, round 1: CPU settings and exact clean-ups","locale":"en"},"12065":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"CPU, round 2: training","locale":"en"},"12066":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#the-attention-dispatcher","content":"The attention dispatcher","locale":"en"},"12067":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-decoding-without-waits-as-cuda-graphs","content":"GPU: decoding without waits, as CUDA graphs","locale":"en"},"12068":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#qwen3-tts","content":"Qwen3-TTS","locale":"en"},"12069":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#fish-speech","content":"Fish Speech","locale":"en"},"12070":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cosyvoice-23","content":"CosyVoice 2/3","locale":"en"},"12071":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#voxcpm","content":"VoxCPM","locale":"en"},"12072":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpt-sovits","content":"GPT-SoVITS","locale":"en"},"12073":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#indextts","content":"IndexTTS","locale":"en"},"12074":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#on-the-cpu","content":"On the CPU","locale":"en"},"12075":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"GPU baseline (G0)","locale":"en"},"12076":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-checks-of-the-cpu-rounds-g1","content":"GPU checks of the CPU rounds (G1)","locale":"en"},"12077":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-optimisation-g2","content":"GPU optimisation (G2)","locale":"en"},"12078":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#against-01-and-the-contract-suite-on-the-gpu-g3","content":"Against 0.1, and the contract suite on the GPU (G3)","locale":"en"},"12079":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#first-audio-and-memory-against-01-g8","content":"First audio and memory against 0.1 (G8)","locale":"en"},"12080":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"Memory estimates calibrated","locale":"en"},"12081":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#attention-backends-on-a-gpu","content":"Attention backends on a GPU","locale":"en"},"12082":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"Partly loaded models","locale":"en"},"12083":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-modes-and-a-real-out-of-memory","content":"Memory modes and a real out-of-memory","locale":"en"},"12084":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#torchcompile-on-the-cpu","content":"`torch.compile` on the CPU","locale":"en"},"12085":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#not-done-because-it-changes-results","content":"Not done because it changes results","locale":"en"},"12086":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance","content":"Speed work on the inference and training code follows one rule: **the results do not change**. Every optimisation recorded here gives the same audio sample for sample, and the same losses and saved weights bit for bit, as the code before it on the CPU, where the engines' parity with upstream is established. On a GPU, \"the same\" means identical to the code before on the same GPU, driver, torch and settings. Faster paths that change the arithmetic are opt-in features, described in Acceleration; the user-facing settings are in Performance.","locale":"en"},"12087":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Tool","locale":"en"},"12088":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"What it does","locale":"en"},"12089":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`scripts/parity/perf.py run <cases.jsonl>… --name NAME [--only ID] [--repeat N] [--device D] [--setting k=v] [--reference NAME] [--profile] [--torch-profile [--keep-trace]] [--deterministic]`","locale":"en"},"12090":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Runs parity cases through the whole stack in one process, each `--repeat` times (3): the first loads the model and prepares the voice, the others are warm. Records the wall time, load, first chunk, engine total, real-time factor, and the time inside each phase (`ctx.use` / `ctx.run`, exclusive of nested phases, plus the time outside phases). Compares the audio sample for sample across the runs and with `--reference`: an earlier run of that name, else the case's parity fixture","locale":"en"},"12091":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`perf.py compare --a NAME --b NAME [--report out.md]`, `perf.py show NAME`","locale":"en"},"12092":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"The warm runs of two rounds side by side, the speed-up, the time to first audio of streamed cases and the phases that changed most","locale":"en"},"12093":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`scripts/parity/train_perf.py run <engine> --fixture F --name NAME [--device D] [--setting k=v] [--reference NAME] [--profile]`, `compare`","locale":"en"},"12094":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"A fine-tune with a parity fixture's recipe through the whole stack, every stage a child process as in use: each stage's time, the time per step, every metric and the SHA-256 of every weight file the export wrote, compared with an earlier run","locale":"en"},"12095":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`--profile` adds an uncounted run under cProfile (in-process stages for `train_perf.py`); `--torch-profile` lists the operators by their own time.","locale":"en"},"12096":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"**On a GPU** (`--device cuda:N`, which becomes every engine's device, and training's): the harness waits for the device at each phase boundary, so a phase's time is its work and not only its launches, and records per phase the peak allocation, the working memory above what was allocated once the phase's weights were placed, and the working memory the engine asked for (with the call that came closest to its own estimate: a phase's largest use and largest estimate can come from different calls), with the request's peak allocated and reserved memory. `--torch-profile` then also records the device's activity and summarises it per phase (`<case>.gpu.json`): wall and device-busy time, kernels, launches, CUDA graph replays, host waits for the device (`cudaStreamSynchronize`, `cudaEventSynchronize`, synchronous `cudaMemcpy`; the harness's own two `cudaDeviceSynchronize` per phase counted apart) and host↔device copies.","locale":"en"},"12097":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`--deterministic` runs with `torch.use_deterministic_algorithms(True, warn_only=True)` and `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG=:4096:8`, set by the harness (native code never sets process-wide state); operations without a deterministic implementation are listed in the record instead of failing the case. `train_perf.py --setting training.memory=low` measures a memory preset.","locale":"en"},"12098":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Records go to `MIZUOTO_PERF_DIR` (default `~/code_workspace/experiment/perf`), one JSON file per case and round.","locale":"en"},"12099":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"A warm time is the fastest warm run: the machine's other work only ever slows a run down. Comparisons that matter are interleaved, the two codes in separate processes, one after the other.","locale":"en"},"12100":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Freeze the code being measured (a worktree at the commit) and run the baseline: `perf.py run` over the case set, `train_perf.py run` for each trainer.","locale":"en"},"12101":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Profile (`--profile`, `--torch-profile`) to find where the time goes; for a kernel-level change, write a micro-benchmark and check the operation's output with `torch.equal` over the shapes the engines use.","locale":"en"},"12102":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Run the changed code with `--reference` set to the baseline: every case must give identical audio, every fine-tune identical metrics and weight hashes. Then run the parity stack for the engines whose code changed, and the test suite.","locale":"en"},"12103":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`perf.py compare` for the table; record the round here.","locale":"en"},"12104":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"The inference set is thirteen cases: Qwen3-TTS `base-clone-en` and `cv-en-aiden`, F5-TTS `clone-en`, OmniVoice `clone-en`, CosyVoice `v2-clone-en` and `v3-clone-en`, GPT-SoVITS `v2-clone-zh` and `v4-clone-zh`, Fish Speech `clone-en`, IndexTTS `v2-clone-en` and `v15-clone-en`, VoxCPM `v2-clone-en` and `v15-clone-en`. The training set is one fine-tune per trainer on an 11-clip dataset.","locale":"en"},"12105":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#conditions","content":"**CPU**: AMD Ryzen 9 5900X (12 cores; torch uses 12 threads), 125 GB RAM, torch 2.8.0 with MKL 2024.2 and oneDNN 3.7.1, Python 3.11; fp32, except VoxCPM in bf16 (its upstream default). Other work on the machine takes about 9 % of its threads; a run whose compute thread is preempted stalls, so single runs vary by up to ±10 %.","locale":"en"},"12106":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#conditions","content":"**GPU**: two RTX 3090 (24 GB), **shared with another workload** that held about 21 GB of each and kept both 100 % busy. Every GPU number below is inflated by that sharing; comparisons were interleaved on the same GPU and torch. Models that did not fit beside the other workload were measured as their decoding loop alone: the real sizes, random weights and a few of their layers, which is labelled where it applies.","locale":"en"},"12107":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#conditions","content":"**GPU, from 2026-10-18** (GPU baseline): the same two RTX 3090, **free** (no other workload), driver 595.99.02, torch 2.8.0+cu128 (CUDA 12.8, cuDNN 9.10.2), PCIe 4.0 x16. Inference ran on one card while the fine-tunes ran on the other, so the two shared the CPU.","locale":"en"},"12108":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"The warm request before the CPU rounds:","locale":"en"},"12109":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Engine, case","locale":"en"},"12110":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Audio s","locale":"en"},"12111":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Warm s","locale":"en"},"12112":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Phases (s)","locale":"en"},"12113":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Qwen3-TTS base-clone-en","locale":"en"},"12114":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"5.44","locale":"en"},"12115":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"34.25","locale":"en"},"12116":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"talker 30.2, codec decoder 4.0","locale":"en"},"12117":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Qwen3-TTS cv-en-aiden","locale":"en"},"12118":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"3.76","locale":"en"},"12119":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"20.72","locale":"en"},"12120":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"talk