ance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"121.2 → 103.4","locale":"en"},"12238":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-178","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"×1.17","locale":"en"},"12239":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-179","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"VoxCPM 0.5B LoRA, 3 steps","locale":"en"},"12240":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-180","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"29.8 → 26.4","locale":"en"},"12241":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-181","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"×1.13","locale":"en"},"12242":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-182","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"GPT-SoVITS v2Pro speaker, 1 + 1 epochs","locale":"en"},"12243":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-183","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"246.0 → 227.3","locale":"en"},"12244":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-184","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"×1.08","locale":"en"},"12245":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-185","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"OmniVoice LoRA, 3 steps","locale":"en"},"12246":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-186","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"39.8 → 36.9","locale":"en"},"12247":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-187","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"×1.08","locale":"en"},"12248":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-188","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"**How the CPU settings were chosen.** Each configuration ran in separate, interleaved processes. Fixing the `mmap` threshold and keeping free heap together helped F5-TTS (warm median 64.0–68.6 s → 53.7–60.9 s); either alone made things slower; glibc's `hugetlb` and tcmalloc gave no more than torch's own huge pages. OmniVoice looked bimodal per process (about 28 s or 38 s, whatever the allocator): the cause was thread placement. Of six processes with default placement one ran at 38.5 s; of six with threads bound to cores none did (27.6–28.3 s).","locale":"en"},"12249":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-189","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"Profiles of the fine-tunes showed backward passes and products as everywhere, AdamW's per-tensor loop, dropout masks, and, for Fish Speech, 41 s of a 123 s training stage spent replaying about 18 billion 32-bit draws from the process-wide generator only to put it where upstream's is when training starts.","locale":"en"},"12250":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-190","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"Change","locale":"en"},"12251":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-191","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"Why it is exact","locale":"en"},"12252":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-192","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"The generator jumped ahead instead of drawing: MT19937's state after *n* draws computed in GF(2) (`mizuoto/training/rng.py`), 204 jumps in 2.3 s instead of 41–52 s","locale":"en"},"12253":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-193","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"the state equals drawing's byte for byte (tested from 1 to 2·10⁹ draws)","locale":"en"},"12254":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-194","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"Fish Speech's LoRA merged with one float32 copy of each base weight, not two","locale":"en"},"12255":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-195","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"the same values","locale":"en"},"12256":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-196","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"Fish Speech's codec `snake` in place when no gradient is taken","locale":"en"},"12257":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-197","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"the same operations in the same order","locale":"en"},"12258":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-198","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"Fish Speech S2 Pro LoRA (2 steps): 207.4 s before the CPU rounds, 172–173 s after round 1, 128.5–130.6 s after round 2 (training stage 146.8 → 83 s), metrics and weights identical. Its warm request went from 110.4 to 85.7 s (the codec 3.3 → 2.0 s), its parity cases unchanged.","locale":"en"},"12259":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-199","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#the-attention-dispatcher","content":"Routing every attention call through `mizuoto.runtime.accel` (Acceleration) costs about 1 µs a call under `auto`: +1.11 µs on a decode-sized call (16 heads, 1 query, 300 keys, head dim 128: 28.8 → 30.0 µs), +0.82 µs on a small one. The most calls a request made in the set is 7,152 (Fish Speech), well under 0.1 % of its time. All thirteen cases and all seven fine-tunes stayed identical; warm times moved within the machine's noise (Fish Speech, interleaved: 86.6/86.9 s before, 86.5/87.3 s after).","locale":"en"},"12260":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-200","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#the-attention-dispatcher","content":"With `compute.attention = fast` on the CPU every call goes through the policy, all on SDPA, no fallbacks (calls per request: F5-TTS 704, OmniVoice 896, GPT-SoVITS v4 3,044, VoxCPM 1.5 3,928, CosyVoice 2 4,688, Qwen3-TTS 4,877, IndexTTS 2 6,667, Fish Speech 7,152). Seven engines still gave identical audio; IndexTTS 2 differed by at most 2 int16 steps (86.1 dB SNR, same length), its perceiver's hand-written softmax being computed by SDPA.","locale":"en"},"12261":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-201","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-decoding-without-waits-as-cuda-graphs","content":"Each autoregressive decoder launched thousands of small kernels per step one by one and waited for the device many times a step (`torch.multinomial`'s checks, masks copied at each step, padding masks read back, stop checks), so the CPU's launch time and the GPU's work added up. Each now waits once per step and, with `compute.cuda_graphs`, replays its step's kernels as CUDA graphs (Acceleration). Every result below is the old code's audio or tokens; the CPU parity cases of each engine stayed identical. All on the shared GPUs described above.","locale":"en"},"12262":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-202","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#qwen3-tts","content":"The decoding loop at the 1.7B model's sizes, random weights, 4 or 8 of its 28 talker layers, 100 frames, batch 1 (the 1.7B model did not fit beside the other workload); ms per frame, best warm run of two interleaved rounds:","locale":"en"},"12263":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-203","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#qwen3-tts","content":"torch","locale":"en"},"12264":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-204","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#qwen3-tts","content":"Configuration","locale":"en"},"12265":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-205","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#qwen3-tts","content":"4 layers","locale":"en"},"12266":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-206","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#qwen3-tts","content":"8 layers","locale":"en"},"12267":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-207","page_id":"