nce-810","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"Measured on the RTX 3090s (2026-10-18), the harness now recording each phase's worst single call (work against its own estimate; a phase's largest work and largest estimate can come from different calls, such as a reference's encoding and a decoding).","locale":"en"},"12871":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-811","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"**Decoders**, bytes per position of output (measured on short and about 30 s texts, then a fifth more): VoxCPM's VAE decoding 1,277 (1.5) and 643 (2) a position against the 256 estimated, its reference encoding 1,395 and 3,100 a sample against 256; Fish Speech's codec 1,922 a sample (the out-of-place Snake since G1 uses more) against 384; IndexTTS's BigVGAN 1,057 (2) and 771 plus 256 MB (1.5) against 256. Since raised, every decoder call, cold or warm, short, long or a batch of 8 (OmniVoice, GPT-SoVITS), stays at or under 82 % of its estimate (IndexTTS 1.5's BigVGAN; Fish's codec 73 %, VoxCPM's VAE 74 %); the closest phase of all is IndexTTS 2's GPT on a long text, 90 %, unchanged and within. Qwen3-TTS 1.7B under an 8 GB cap loads whole; under 4 GB its talker loads partly, and `meta.memory.partial` and the log line say which component, why and with what budget.","locale":"en"},"12872":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-812","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"**Training**, each method with its recipe's default batch (G1 had used the fixtures' smaller ones), peak MB by preset:","locale":"en"},"12873":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-813","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"Fine-tune","locale":"en"},"12874":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-814","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"`standard`","locale":"en"},"12875":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-815","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"`low`","locale":"en"},"12876":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-816","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"`lowest`","locale":"en"},"12877":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-817","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"CosyVoice 2 / 3 `llm` (and 2's `llm_flow`)","locale":"en"},"12878":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-818","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"10,319 / 10,398","locale":"en"},"12879":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-819","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"9,691 / 9,693","locale":"en"},"12880":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-820","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"9,691 / 9,693","locale":"en"},"12881":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-821","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#memory-estimates-calibrated","content":"F5-TTS `full`","locale":"en"},"12882":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performanc