t-testing-361","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Fails when a job above failed or was cancelled, passes when they passed or were skipped: the one check to require in branch protection","locale":"en"},"13873":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-362","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"A newer run of the same branch cancels the older one. A push to `main` that changes a version file starts the release workflow, which runs `ci.yml` as its checks for what the push changed; CI's own run of that push skips every job (`scope` sees the version file), so the checks run once. Tags start only the release workflow, whose checks are everything. Pushing the docs site to GitHub Pages is `site.yml`'s, not CI's (see Release).","locale":"en"},"13874":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-363","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`python scripts/dev.py check` runs all of it locally (on your platform and Python only, with the gated packages installed, on Mizuoto at your checkout). The contract suite and the parity harness need models (and, for parity, the reference environments) weighing tens of gigabytes, so they stay out of CI; the release workflow has its own wheel smoke test (see Release).","locale":"en"},"13875":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-364","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"CI has only a CPU build of torch. The GPU paths (CUDA graphs, the optional attention backends, weight streaming's asynchronous prefetch) are tested there for their logic; how they behave on a GPU has to be checked on a machine with one, with the contract suite and the parity harness.","locale":"en"},"13876":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"page","content":"Trainers","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training","locale":"en"},"13877":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training","content":"How training methods are declared in a manifest, how datasets are added, prepared and checked, how a method's stages run one by one as child processes of Mizuoto's own interpreter, how fingerprints decide what is stale, how trainers are written, and what exports register.","locale":"en"},"13878":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Training methods in the manifest","locale":"en"},"13879":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#datasets","content":"Datasets","locale":"en"},"13880":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#experiments-fingerprints-and-stale-stages","content":"Experiments, fingerprints and stale stages","locale":"en"},"13881":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#the-train-job","content":"The train job","locale":"en"},"13882":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#why-a-child-process","content":"Why a child process","locale":"en"},"13883":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#trainer-and-stagecontext","content":"Trainer and StageContext","locale":"en"},"13884":{"id":"